  "workload": "Ring plus deterministic long-range edge per node; query-wide phase, remove/replace edge burst, direct-neighbor local-query phase, second remove/replace burst, wide-query phase. Exact reachability is recomputed by BFS on the current graph, so deletions cannot leave stale positive/negative closure facts.",
  "methods": "set BFS; epoch-stamped uint32-array BFS; adaptive BFS controller that measures both strategies for initial calibration and periodically probes both, updates exponential moving-average costs, and switches route. Graph storage: Python sets vs per-node array(I) compact adjacency. Each process generates its own deterministic workload, so graph payload is not transferred between parent/child.",
  "memory": "RSS sampled from /proc/self/status before/after graph build and after workload; process peak RSS from resource.ru_maxrss. Retained adjacency Python heap estimate counts container and neighbor object sizes. Values include interpreter/runtime; resident memory deltas are more informative than peak RSS for the graph structure.",
  "energy": "not measured; no joules inferred. FLOPs not applicable to this integer graph workload and not fabricated.",
  "limitations": [
    "Synthetic workload, five seeds default; not an externally representative graph dataset.",
    "100k-node cases use sparse degree-two base graph and 90 queries, not dense or adversarial worst case.",
    "Deletion correctness comes from search over current adjacency rather than a fully dynamic transitive-closure algorithm.",
    "Online controller adapts query traversal only; storage backend is fixed per run.",
    "Compact arrays support unsigned 32-bit node identifiers."
